{"reason": "The initial plan focuses on reducing overfitting via data augmentation and SWA, which aligns with observed validation fluctuation (0.0659–0.1162). Historical successful experiments used DeBERTa-v3-large with differential learning rates and achieved good results without complex feature engineering, so I avoid adding feature engineering. The most promising modules to modify are data_processing_and_feature_engineering (to implement data augmentation) and training_evaluation (to implement SWA). Model design is kept unchanged as the architecture already benefits from historical best practices.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Add a text augmentation function using WordNet-based synonym replacement. For each training sentence, randomly replace 10-15% of non-stopword tokens with a synonym (randomly chosen from WordNet synsets). To ensure interface compatibility, the augmentation will be applied on-the-fly in the SpookyDataset __getitem__ with a probability (e.g., 0.5) during training, preserving the original dataset class and returning tokenized inputs as before. This increases lexical diversity based on historical success of data augmentation in text classification.", "training_evaluation": "Implement Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) starting from epoch 15, with a learning rate of 1e-6 and SWA update cycle length of 5 epochs. After training, replace final model weights with SWA averaged weights. This smooths validation oscillation and improves generalization, as historical experiments show that averaging multiple points in the loss landscape yields more robust models. The training loop will be modified to maintain a separate SWA model copy and update it every 5 epochs after epoch 15, then finally load the SWA averaged state dict for inference."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
