{"reason": "The initial plan identifies training instability, weak augmentation, and uniform loss focus as key issues. Historical experiments show that multi-stage fine-tuning (frozen head then unfrozen backbone) successfully stabilizes training, and back-translation augmentation is preferred over synonym replacement for preserving author style. However, the validation plateau at ~0.069 suggests the current model is already competitive but needs marginal gains. Given the successful historical experiment with OneCycleLR and multi-pooling (achieving validation log-loss below 0.10), and that the current training uses CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts which may cause instability, I select model_design to replace current [CLS] pooling with MHA+Mean-Max concatenation (proven to capture author style better), and training_evaluation to replace the current scheduler with OneCycleLR and implement two-stage fine-tuning as per the initial plan. Data processing retains augmentation but swaps to back-translation per initial plan. These changes directly address root causes without overcomplication.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation", "data_processing_and_feature_engineering"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the current mean pooling in forward() with a Multi-Head Attention (MHA) + Mean-Max Concatenation pooling module. After backbone, apply a learned MHA (hidden_size, num_heads=8) over token hidden states, then compute mean and max pooling across sequence length, concatenate all three representations (3 * hidden_size), apply a linear projection to 256 (hidden_dim) with dropout=0.3, then pass to the classification head. This change, validated by historical success with DeBERTa-v3-large, captures author style from the entire text more effectively than simple mean pooling, targeting the plateau in validation loss.", "training_evaluation": "Implement the two-stage fine-tuning: Stage 1 (first 10% of total steps) freezes backbone entirely and trains only the classification head (intermediate + classifier) with a warmup schedule; Stage 2 unfreezes last 8 layers and continues with OneCycleLR (max_lr=2e-5 for backbone, 5e-5 for head, pct_start=0.1). Replace the current CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts with OneCycleLR to reduce training variance and ensure stable convergence, as observed in successful historical experiments. Also, integrate Focal Loss with adaptive gamma (initial gamma=2.0, increase by 0.5 if val log loss does not improve for 3 epochs, max gamma=4.0) instead of standard CrossEntropyLoss with label smoothing, focusing on hard examples per initial plan.", "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Replace the current on-the-fly WordNet synonym augmentation with offline pre-computed back-translation augmentation. For each training sentence, generate three augmented versions using Helsinki-NLP OpusMT models (English-to-French, English-to-German, English-to-Spanish to English) with 30% probability. Pre-compute and store augmented texts to avoid memory overhead. This change, informed by historical experiments where synonym augmentation was insufficient and back-translation preserved author style, increases data diversity naturally and addresses the root cause of weak augmentation."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan identifies training instability, weak augmentation, and uniform loss focus as key issues. Historical experiments show that multi-stage fine-tuning (frozen head then unfrozen backbone) successfully stabilizes training, and back-translation augmentation is preferred over synonym replacement for preserving author style. However, the validation plateau at ~0.069 suggests the current model is already competitive but needs marginal gains. Given the successful historical experiment with OneCycleLR and multi-pooling (achieving validation log-loss below 0.10), and that the current training uses CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts which may cause instability, I select model_design to replace current [CLS] pooling with MHA+Mean-Max concatenation (proven to capture author style better), and training_evaluation to replace the current scheduler with OneCycleLR and implement two-stage fine-tuning as per the initial plan. Data processing retains augmentation but swaps to back-translation per initial plan. These changes directly address root causes without overcomplication.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation", "data_processing_and_feature_engineering"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the current mean pooling in forward() with a Multi-Head Attention (MHA) + Mean-Max Concatenation pooling module. After backbone, apply a learned MHA (hidden_size, num_heads=8) over token hidden states, then compute mean and max pooling across sequence length, concatenate all three representations (3 * hidden_size), apply a linear projection to 256 (hidden_dim) with dropout=0.3, then pass to the classification head. This change, validated by historical success with DeBERTa-v3-large, captures author style from the entire text more effectively than simple mean pooling, targeting the plateau in validation loss.",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement the two-stage fine-tuning: Stage 1 (first 10% of total steps) freezes backbone entirely and trains only the classification head (intermediate + classifier) with a warmup schedule; Stage 2 unfreezes last 8 layers and continues with OneCycleLR (max_lr=2e-5 for backbone, 5e-5 for head, pct_start=0.1). Replace the current CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts with OneCycleLR to reduce training variance and ensure stable convergence, as observed in successful historical experiments. Also, integrate Focal Loss with adaptive gamma (initial gamma=2.0, increase by 0.5 if val log loss does not improve for 3 epochs, max gamma=4.0) instead of standard CrossEntropyLoss with label smoothing, focusing on hard examples per initial plan.",
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Replace the current on-the-fly WordNet synonym augmentation with offline pre-computed back-translation augmentation. For each training sentence, generate three augmented versions using Helsinki-NLP OpusMT models (English-to-French, English-to-German, English-to-Spanish to English) with 30% probability. Pre-compute and store augmented texts to avoid memory overhead. This change, informed by historical experiments where synonym augmentation was insufficient and back-translation preserved author style, increases data diversity naturally and addresses the root cause of weak augmentation."
  }
}"}
